    "text": "To solve the problem, we start with the equation \\(\\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{x}{3} = b\\). We aim to find the positive value of \\(b\\) such that the equation has exactly one solution for \\(x\\).\n\nFirst, we eliminate the fractions by multiplying both sides of the equation by \\(3x\\):\n\\[3x \\left( \\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{x}{3} \\right) = 3x \\cdot b.\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[3 \\cdot 3 + x \\cdot x = 3bx,\\]\nwhich further simplifies to:\n\\[9 + x^2 = 3bx.\\]\nRearranging the terms gives us a standard quadratic equation:\n\\[x^2 - 3bx + 9 = 0.\\]\nFor the quadratic equation \\(x^2 - 3bx + 9 = 0\\) to have exactly one solution, its discriminant must be zero. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of a quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) is given by \\(\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\\). Here, \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -3b\\), and \\(c = 9\\). So, the discriminant is:\n\\[\\Delta = (-3b)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 9 = 9b^2 - 36.\\]\nSetting the discriminant to zero, we get:\n\\[9b^2 - 36 = 0.\\]\nSolving for \\(b\\), we have:\n\\[9b^2 = 36,\\]\n\\[b^2 = 4,\\]\n\\[b = 2 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad b = -2.\\]\nSince we are asked for the positive value of \\(b\\), we have:\n\\[b = 2.\\]\nThus, the positive value of \\(b\\) such that the equation \\(\\frac{3}{x} + \\frac{x}{3} = b\\) has exactly one solution is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
